WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
In virtual reality, posture affects comfort, immersion, and performance.
Most experiences assume you are either sitting or standing.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:18.000
But what if posture support allowed natural transitions between both?
We explored this using a passive lower-body exoskeleton chair designed to support seated-to-standing movement in VR.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:30.000
Participants completed a Unity-based scooter task where posture controlled speed while collecting coins in an immersive environment.
Three conditions were compared: seated, unsupported standing, and exoskeleton-supported posture.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Standing increased physical load and fatigue.
Seated posture improved performance and reduced nausea.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:54.000
The exoskeleton reduced fatigue compared to standing,
but revealed a trade-off between comfort and sensory stability.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:02.000
These findings raise important design questions for embodied virtual reality systems.
